{"name":"Cocoa Berry","hex":"#a08882","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cocoa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The dried and partially fermented fatty seeds of the cacao tree from which chocolate is made.","An unsweetened brown powder made from roasted, ground cocoa beans, used in making chocolate, and in cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light to medium brown colour, like that of cocoa powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cocoa"},{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry","wikidata":"Q16128920"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/250px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Norwegian_blueberry.jpg/500px-Norwegian_blueberry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x